const faqs = [
 { q: 'What is screen printing?', a: 'Screen printing is a decoration method that pushes ink through a fine mesh stencil onto fabric, layer by layer for each color. The ink cures into the fibers so the design is durable, vivid, and washable. It\'s the standard for t-shirts, hoodies, and high-volume apparel runs.' },
 { q: 'How does screen printing work step by step?', a: 'A digital design is separated by color and prepared as screens. Ink is applied through the screens, then cured under the selected ink manufacturer\'s documented time, temperature, equipment, garment, and process requirements.' },
 { q: 'Is screen printing better than embroidery?', a: 'For tees, hoodies, and large/colorful designs at volume, yes. Screen printing prints flat onto fabric so big designs and gradients work, and per-piece cost falls fast at quantity because most cost is in setup. Embroidery wins on polos, button-downs, hats, and any garment where a premium textured look matters more than design size or color.' },
 { q: 'What fabrics work with screen printing?', a: 'The fabric, coating, ink, and artwork decide whether it can be printed. Request product-specific approval and written minimums.' },
 { q: 'How many colors can you screen print?', a: 'The artwork, garment, and ink set the color count, setup, and cost. Your proof and quote confirm it.' },
 { q: 'How long does screen printed ink last?', a: 'Ink, garment, cure, and laundering set how long a print lasts. Confirm the approved care instructions for the order.' },
