 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-700">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">Screen printing pushes ink through a mesh stencil onto a compatible product, then cures the ink. It can suit tees, hoodies, and larger designs. The order, the use, and the care set the price and how long it lasts.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="how-it-works" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How Screen Printing Actually Works</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The process has been around for decades but the underlying mechanics haven't changed much. <a href="https://www.britannica.com/topic/silk-screen-printing" rel="noopener" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">Silk screen printing</a>, as it's traditionally called, has six stages:</p>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Color separation.</strong> The design is broken into separate layers, one per ink color.</li>
 <li><strong>Screen burning.</strong> Each color layer is exposed onto a fine mesh screen coated in light-sensitive emulsion. Where the design blocks the light, the emulsion stays soft and washes out, leaving open mesh that ink can pass through.</li>
 <li><strong>Press setup.</strong> Screens are mounted on a manual or automatic press. The garment is loaded onto a platen aligned under the screens.</li>
 <li><strong>Ink + squeegee pass.</strong> Ink is flooded across the screen, then pulled through the open mesh with a squeegee onto the garment. One pass per color.</li>
 <li><strong>Layer registration.</strong> For multi-color designs, the garment passes under each screen in sequence. Registration marks ensure colors line up exactly.</li>
 <li><strong>Cure.</strong> The printed garment passes through a heat tunnel at ~320°F (160°C). The ink cures, which is what lets it survive the wash.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For deeper background see our piece on <a href="/blog/plastisol-vs-water-based-ink-workwear/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">plastisol vs water-based ink</a>, which covers the two main ink families and which one fits which use case.</p>

 <h2 id="when-it-wins" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">When Screen Printing Wins</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Screen printing is the right call for:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Larger identical runs.</strong> Setup gets spread across the run, so more shirts usually means a lower price per shirt.</li>
 <li><strong>Large designs.</strong> Full-back prints, oversized chest logos, anything that wouldn't fit cleanly in embroidery.</li>
 <li><strong>Bold, flat designs.</strong> Block letters, simple shapes, high-contrast logos.</li>
 <li><strong>Cotton and cotton-blend tees and hoodies.</strong> The fabric the process was built for.</li>
 <li><strong>Field crew apparel.</strong> Construction, landscaping, event staff: anywhere the durability matters more than the premium feel.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="when-it-loses" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">When Embroidery Wins Instead</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Pick <a href="/services/embroidery-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">embroidery</a> over screen printing for:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Polos, button-downs, hats, jackets.</strong> The premium feel of stitched thread reads "established business," not "team event."</li>
 <li><strong>Smaller batches.</strong> Compare current embroidery and printing setup, minimums, and total quoted cost.</li>
 <li><strong>Performance fabrics.</strong> Polyester polos take embroidery cleanly without dye-migration concerns.</li>
 <li><strong>Multi-color logos with fine detail.</strong> Embroidery handles fine lines and small text better than screen printing on knit fabrics.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Most companies run both: embroidered polos for client-facing roles, <a href="/screen-printing-vs-embroidery-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">screen-printed tees for the field crew</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="costs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">What Drives Screen Printing Cost</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Three factors:</p>
 <ol class="mb-6 list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Setup per color.</strong> Each color is a separate screen burn + press setup, charged per color. A 4-color design has 4x the setup of a 1-color design.</li>
 <li><strong>Garment cost.</strong> A blank Carhartt K87 wholesale is very different from a Gildan 2000.</li>
 <li><strong>Quantity.</strong> Per-piece printing falls dramatically with volume. 24 pieces vs. 200 pieces vs. 500 pieces gives radically different per-piece pricing because setup is fixed.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For a tighter look at pricing, see <a href="/blog/how-much-to-embroider-a-shirt/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">how embroidery pricing works</a>. Many of the same factors apply.</p>

 <h2 id="fabric" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Fabric Compatibility</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">The fabric, coating, ink, and artwork decide whether it can be printed. Product categories to review include:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>100% cotton tees.</strong> The classic substrate. Crisp prints, full color saturation, fast cure.</li>
 <li><strong>50/50 cotton-poly.</strong> Slightly different ink pickup but identical results in practice.</li>
 <li><strong>100% polyester.</strong> We check the ink, preparation, cure, and dye-migration controls.</li>
 <li><strong>Tri-blends.</strong> Cotton/poly/rayon, softer hand, prints fine but fabric texture means slight ink absorption variation.</li>
 <li><strong>Hi-vis poly mesh.</strong> Print placement is constrained by the <a href="https://safetyequipment.org/standards/ansi-isea-107/" rel="noopener" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">ANSI 107 retroreflective area limits</a>, but the fabric prints cleanly.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="durability" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">How Long Does It Last</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Ink, garment, cure, and laundering set print life. Follow the written instructions for the approved product and decoration.</p>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal"></p>

